rest (18 seconds of /uniFB01 xation). During the encoding phase, participants were /uniFB01 rst instructed to determine whether each aversive or neutral picture represented an indoor or outdoor scene. Participants were not informed about the subsequent retrieval phase before scanning, and thus they did not realize that they were engaged in a memory task. Approximately 2 minutes after the encoding phase, participants had to remember whether the image presented was seen during the encoding session in the case of mixing with half new pictures and respond ' new ' or ' old ' via a button press. Finally, both reaction time (RT) and accuracy (ACC) in the encoding and retrieval phase were extracted from log /uniFB01 les of task performance after scanning. The schematic diagram of the task is shown in Figure 2A, and a detailed description is provided in the Supplement.

## Image Acquisition and Preprocessing

Imaging data were acquired from participants on a 3T GE Discovery MR750 scanner in the Center for MRI Research, Peking University (healthy dataset) and the Center for Neuroimaging, Peking University Sixth Hospital (clinical dataset). The high-resolution structural T1-weighted MRI and fMRI blood oxygen level -dependent signal were acquired and preprocessed using SPM12 (http://www. /uniFB01 l.ion.ucl.ac.uk/spm) with a standardized protocol (61). The details of imaging acquisition and preprocessing can be found in the Supplement.

